ries and managing the  whole data management lifecycle and we  produce lots of things dealing with  entomological data archaeological data  earth sciences data all sorts of things  we have work from historians look  working the natural text processing to  sort of base tools having to do with you  know real problems in basic data munging  issues of working between formats and  such so we produce a lot of these is a  big community of authors and at a  certain point as this expand and  expanded and as our author base expanded  we needed to figure out how to validate  and quality control all of the things  entering our repository in our  collection and being primarily academic  researchers we come out of a tradition  of sort of academic peer review an  academic peer review is a little bit  different from what may be common in  sort of an end software engineering  space which is often sort of production  code review so when one reviews code in  a production software development  environment it tends to be a granular  process where people submit patches or  pull requests to repositories often  what's being reviewed is narrow in scope  you know you've got one function you've  got one change you've got one or a  refactor and there's a few specific  things that you're looking at within  that and the people are reviewing that  are generally ones project collaborators  people who know you well and people who  know the project well so they can  understand the context that it goes in  and the reason one does this is  primarily quality control because you  want to make sure that you're not  introducing bugs or introducing  conflicts into your program but those of  us who come out of an academic research  tradition tend to be more familiar with  the idea of academic peer review which  is a different social structure  essentially so when one reviews  something in an academic peer review  environment it's generally so  a project-wide review one is usually  submitting a manuscript that may  represent years and years and work of a  group and the reviewers have a mandate  to review everything in it from methods  to theory to data to the quality of the  presentation and generally the people  are reviewing it our peers in your field  who are external to your group often  anonymous peers often mediated by some  sort of editorial process and the  purpose is to measure quality to you  know remove problematic components of  your work to improve its quality but  also to confer the legitimacy of other  people in your fields to give a stamp of  approval so that when it goes out into  the world other people can feel that  it's validated so we decided to draw on  components of both of these because many  of our developers come from this  academic tradition but also many of them  work in small to single person groups  they're often the only developer within  an otherwise research group and the idea  of having external peers review their  code appeal to a great many of them  because they wanted to reach out to the  broader community for both for both  wisdom and also that validity you can  also sort of think of this as a spectrum  where on one side you have very frequent  iteration within a team that has very  high familiarity with a project and at  the most extreme end that's basically  pair programming when you have two  people or more sitting side-by-side  working on the code together to the  other extreme where you have this  project level peer review and then in  the middle you may have sort of this  patch or pull request approach so why  did we go about this peer review and  what are we able to do it do with it  well there are three goals we wanted in  the design of the system one is to drive  adoption of best practices and standards  again our pool of data scientists and  developers often do this as a secondary  job as part of their main sort of a  research goal so  they're not necessarily trained in  software engineering and therefore don't  necessari